\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate STA along three axes: ISR as a diagnostic for reward hacking (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}); ISR as a training reward, where it exhibits Goodhart effects (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}); and cross-domain generalization across logic types and dataset families (\S\ref{sec:crossdomain}).
A hacking taxonomy synthesizes the modes observed across conditions (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

\subsection{Setup}\label{sec:setup}

We train Qwen3-4B~\citep{qwen3} with GRPO~\citep{deepseekmath} ($K{=}4$, lr $10^{-6}$, 500 steps default, 1000 where noted) over seeds $\{42,43,44\}$ (ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ extends to $\{45,46\}$; Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}) on a fixed 500-example FOLIO~\citep{folio} subset held constant across all conditions; the SFT baseline uses negative log-likelihood on gold formalizations.\footnote{Seeds control initialization and rollout sampling; all conditions share the same fixed training subset.}
Held-out evaluation uses 100 FOLIO examples with greedy decoding;\footnote{Seed~43 uses nucleus sampling ($T{=}0.7$) due to greedy-mode degeneration; excluding s43 yields consistent or stronger collapse: at 1000 steps, both remaining seeds show ${\leq}7\%$ non-hacking (Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}).} all cross-run ISR values are recomputed uniformly by a single evaluator so training-log ISR is never compared across runs.
Cross-dataset experiments (\S\ref{sec:crossdomain}) use ProofFOL, a silver-standard FOL corpus of ${\sim}$500 training examples constructed by translating ProofWriter~\citep{proofwriter} problems via GPT-4o and filtering through Prover9 (${\sim}70\%$ retention; Appendix~\ref{app:prooffol-construction}); \emph{stable convergence} denotes $\overline{\text{PR}}_{\text{final\,50}} > 0.90$.
We report prove rate (PR), proved-only ISR ($\text{ISR}^*$), hacking-mode rates from a two-stage classifier (strict text matching for CE/TS plus functional analysis of the per-sample $n_{\text{necessary}}$ distribution; \S\ref{sec:taxonomy-method}; classifier blind spots discussed in Appendix~\ref{app:extended-discussion}), and a \emph{non-hacking} (N-H) rate, an upper bound on genuine reasoning: the residual matching none of the eight cataloged patterns (classifier recall unknown; 152-sample precision audit in Appendix~\ref{app:hacking-modes}).

\paragraph{Statistical considerations.}
With three seeds exhibiting multi-attractor dynamics, per-seed qualitative analysis is the primary framework; the divergence itself is a finding.
Pooled CMH $p$-values ($N{=}3$ strata) are descriptive summaries, not formal inference; non-significant comparisons are exploratory.
An $N{=}2$ sensitivity analysis excluding s43 confirms consistent or stronger patterns (Appendix~\ref{app:n2-sensitivity}).
ProofFOL results ($p{=}0.10$, $N{=}3$) are exploratory and underpowered.

\subsection{ISR as Diagnostic}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

A neurosymbolic reasoner can achieve rising prove rate while the internal structure of its proofs collapses entirely.
The root cause is evaluation by a single criterion---whether the generated formalization proves under a formal solver---which treats all proofs as equal: a formalization that embeds the conclusion verbatim among its premises receives the same reward as one that derives it through genuine reasoning.
Under reinforcement learning, this indifference becomes an active vulnerability~\citep{geirhos2020shortcut}; the optimizer exploits any shortcut the reward fails to penalize~\citep{skalse-reward-hacking, reward-hacking-survey}, and execution feedback provides no signal against structural shortcuts.

Neurosymbolic formalizers~\citep{logic-lm, linc, satlm} and RL-trained variants~\citep{mellgren-prolog-grpo, logic-rl, deepseekmath} rely on execution accuracy alone, with no diagnostic for whether individual components contribute to the proof.
Process reward models~\citep{lightman-verify-step, math-shepherd} offer finer granularity but require expensive step-level annotations that do not transfer to solver-backed settings, while inference-time gaming detection~\citep{kim-game-formalization, bondarenko-spec-gaming} identifies gaming post-hoc but provides no training-time structural signal.
What is missing is an annotation-free method for testing \emph{component necessity} in neurosymbolic outputs.

We address this gap with \emph{Solver Trace Ablation} (STA), a diagnostic that tests whether each component of a formalization is necessary for proof success.
For each proved output, STA removes components one at a time and re-runs the solver; the fraction whose removal breaks the proof yields the \emph{Intensional Structural Reward} (ISR).
A formalization where every component is necessary receives $\text{ISR}{=}1$; one that proves regardless of which component is removed receives $\text{ISR}{=}0$.
Leave-one-out (LOO) ablation itself is standard~\citep{demillo1978, jia-harman-survey}; our contribution is its systematic application to neurosymbolic outputs, where it reveals a diagnostic--optimization asymmetry (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}--\ref{sec:goodhart}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/fig5_training_isr_curves.pdf}
\caption{ISR$^*$ (proved samples only) training trajectories across reward conditions (rolling $w{=}20$). Exec-only collapses to $\text{ISR}{\approx}0$, confirming structural degeneration (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}); the ${\sim}0.5$ plateau is an $n{=}2$ artifact. ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ seeds rise toward $\text{ISR}{\approx}1$ via TS gaming (Goodhart effect, \S\ref{sec:goodhart}); random remains low.}
\label{fig:training-isr-curves}
\end{figure}

Applied post-hoc, ISR reveals that rising prove rate conceals a compositional shift in proof structure: genuine reasoning gives way to hacking modes---conclusion embedding and tautological shortcuts---invisible to execution-based metrics (Figure~\ref{fig:training-isr-curves}; \S\ref{sec:diagnostic}).

Yet the same LOO structure that makes ISR diagnostic also creates a Goodhart vulnerability~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy, karwowski-goodhart-rl, gao-reward-overoptimization}: tautological formalizations ($P{\to}P$) satisfy the necessity criterion by construction, so when ISR is repurposed as a training reward, the model learns to game the diagnostic rather than the task.
This is not an isolated failure of one metric---it exposes a structural risk inherent to the RLVR paradigm: \emph{any} verifiable proxy that rewards structural properties of solver inputs is susceptible to the same exploitation.
In our Qwen3-4B experiments, the asymmetry persists across logic types (FOL, Prolog), dataset families (FOLIO, ProofFOL), reward doses, and random seeds, suggesting that structural proxies for formalization integrity are easier to observe than to optimize.

We contribute:
(\textbf{C1})~STA, a diagnostic method applicable to any solver-verified reasoning system, including the ISR metric, an eight-mode hacking taxonomy, and a cross-domain evaluation protocol (\S\S\ref{sec:sta}--\ref{sec:crossdomain});
(\textbf{C2})~empirical characterization of a seed-dependent Goodhart effect when ISR is repurposed as reward, with matched controls ruling out confounds (\S\ref{sec:goodhart});
(\textbf{C3})~cross-domain replication: the Goodhart effect transfers across logic types (FOL/Prolog) and dataset families (FOLIO/ProofFOL), establishing that structural degeneration under RLVR is not dataset-specific (\S\ref{sec:crossdomain}).

